# Caddy in front of the ingest endpoint. Copy to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile and replace the hostname.
#
# Replace <IP-WITH-DASHES> with the host's external IPv4, dashes for dots: 203.0.113.9 becomes
# 203-0-113-9. sslip.io resolves that name to that address, so Let's Encrypt issues for it over
# HTTP-01 with no domain to buy and no DNS account to create.
#
# Reserve the address as static FIRST. The hostname embeds the IP, so an ephemeral address that
# changes on stop/start silently invalidates every AGENT_KNOWLEDGE_INGEST already committed to a
# project's .claude/settings.json — and the symptom is every member's hook spooling with nobody
# noticing. On GCP: gcloud compute addresses create <name> --addresses <ip> --region <region>.
#
# Why a real certificate and not self-signed: basic auth sends the member credential on every note
# in a reversible form. A client that cannot verify the certificate has to be told to stop
# verifying, and that switch lives in the hook's environment — in a committed settings.json — so it
# would travel to the whole team by git pull. See the README section on authentication.

<IP-WITH-DASHES>.sslip.io {
	# The endpoint binds 127.0.0.1, so nothing but this proxy can reach it. That is deliberate twice
	# over: a wrong firewall rule cannot expose the node process, and a loopback bind means the
	# operator never has to assert --behind-tls-proxy, which is a promise the endpoint has no way to
	# check.
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8791
}
